---
import AdSlot from "../../shared/AdSlot.astro";
import Layout from "../../shared/Layout.astro";
import SandboxFrame from "../../shared/SandboxFrame.astro";
import { toolJsonLd } from "../../shared/seo";
import { toolBySlug } from "../../tools/registry";

const tool = toolBySlug("csv-precheck");
const source = "https://github.com/xixiaofinland/salesforce-tools/blob/main";
---

<Layout
  title={`${tool.name}: Check Your CSV Before Import`}
  description={tool.description}
  jsonLd={toolJsonLd(tool, Astro.site!.href)}
>
  <h1>{tool.name}</h1>
  <p>
    Open a CSV file to check it. The file is opened in your browser and never leaves your computer.
    Don't take our word for it: disconnect from the internet and try again, it still works.
  </p>
  <SandboxFrame tool={tool.slug} title={tool.name} />
  <AdSlot name="csv-precheck-below-tool" />

  <!-- Every claim about Data Loader here comes from docs/research/data-loader-csv-facts.md. -->
  <section class="content">
    <h2>What it checks</h2>
    <p>
      The checks follow how Data Loader's own CSV reader handles a file, taken from its open-source
      code and Salesforce's help articles.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Encoding.</strong> The file must be UTF-8. It flags bytes in another encoding, which
        can garble accented letters, and a byte order mark at the start, which a Salesforce help
        article says Data Loader rejects; whether current versions still do is unconfirmed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Delimiter.</strong> It works out whether the file uses commas, semicolons or tabs.
        Data Loader reads a semicolon or tab file only if you choose that delimiter in its settings,
        and the Bulk API accepts nothing but commas.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Header row.</strong> Data Loader skips blank lines and reads the first remaining
        line as the field names. A file with no such line cannot be loaded.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Empty header cells</strong>, including the ones a stray delimiter at the end of the
        header line creates.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Duplicate column names.</strong> Data Loader warns when two names match exactly. Its
        field mapping also ignores case, so <code>Name</code> and <code>name</code> share one entry
        and cannot be mapped to fields separately.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Unclosed quotes.</strong> A value that opens a double quote and never closes it
        swallows the rest of the file.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Column counts.</strong> Every row must have exactly as many columns as the header. A
        value holding the delimiter or a line break without double quotes around it is a common
        cause.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Salesforce IDs.</strong> It finds the ID columns by name, such as
        <code>Id</code> or <code>AccountId</code>, or because most of their values look like IDs. An
        ID must be 15 or 18 letters and digits; it also flags 18-character IDs whose last three
        characters do not match the first 15, columns mixing both lengths, 15-character IDs that
        differ only in letter case, and empty values in an <code>Id</code> column. One click
        converts every 15-character ID to 18 characters, which do not depend on letter case.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dates.</strong> It finds the date columns by their values and checks each value
        against the formats Data Loader accepts, such as <code>yyyy-MM-dd</code> and
        <code>yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss.SSS+0000</code>, rejecting days that do not exist, such as
        <code>2024-02-30</code>, and dates outside 1700 to 4000. Tick
        <strong>Use European date format</strong> to match the same setting in Data Loader: slash
        dates are then read as <code>dd/MM/yyyy</code> instead of <code>MM/dd/yyyy</code>. A value
        such as <code>13/02/2024</code> shows which order a column uses, so it flags a column in the
        other order from the setting, and warns when every value reads either way. It also warns
        about offsets written with a colon, such as <code>+01:00</code>, and about times in date
        columns, which can shift a date by a day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Spaces.</strong> Data Loader does not trim values, so it lists the columns with
        values that start or end with a space, and any space next to a double quote, as in
        <code>"John", "Smith"</code>, which the Bulk API rejects. It never changes a value itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Multi-line values.</strong> It notes the columns where values hold line breaks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Repeated rows.</strong> Rows identical in every column, which would load as
        duplicate records.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Repeated key values.</strong> Pick a key column, such as the external ID you upsert
        on, to list the values that appear in more than one row. Values are compared exactly, or
        optionally ignoring letter case and surrounding spaces; empty values are never counted.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Size.</strong> The Bulk API takes at most 10,000 records and 10 MB in one batch. A
        file over either limit gets a note, and you can split it into files of as many rows as you
        choose, each with the header row, downloaded together as a zip.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Data Loader errors it helps prevent</h2>
    <p>
      These are Data Loader's messages for the problems above, word for word. The empty and
      duplicate column warnings appear as you choose the file and ask whether to continue; the
      others stop the load.
    </p>
    <ul class="errors">
      <li>
        <code>
          CSV Error: Invalid CSV file format. Please select a different file. Error getting header
          row from the CSV file
        </code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>Error getting header row from the CSV file.</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>An empty column is found in the header row</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>A duplicate column [Name] is found</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>EOF reached before closing an opened quote</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>
          Error reading data row #2: the number of columns (5) exceeds the number of columns in the
          header (4)
        </code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>
          Error reading data row #1: the number of columns (3) is less than the number of columns in
          the header (4)
        </code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>MALFORMED_ID: Object name id: id value of incorrect type</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>MALFORMED_ID: bad id</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>MISSING_ARGUMENT: Id not specified in an update call</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>Error converting value to correct data type: Failed to parse date: 2024-02-30</code>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A row with the wrong number of columns stops the whole operation, so no row after it loads.
      The report lists every such row at once, instead of one per failed run.
    </p>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h3>Is my file uploaded?</h3>
    <p>
      No. The file is opened in your browser and read there. The tool runs in a sandboxed frame
      whose content security policy blocks every network request, so it has no way to send your data
      anywhere. To verify it yourself: load this page, disconnect from the internet, then open your
      file. The check still works. The code is open source: see the
      <a href={`${source}/src/pages/sandbox/csv-precheck.astro`}>tool's page</a> and its
      <a href={`${source}/src/shared/sandbox/csp.ts`}>security policy</a> on GitHub.
    </p>
    <h3>Does it change my file?</h3>
    <p>
      No. It only reads the file. You can download the report as a text file, split the file into
      new, smaller files, or download a copy with 18-character IDs; the CSV itself is left as it is.
      Split files keep every row exactly as written, in its original order, and the converted copy
      differs only in the IDs.
    </p>
    <h3>Why is Data Loader's row number one less than the report's?</h3>
    <p>
      Data Loader counts data rows from 0 and leaves out blank lines, so the first row under the
      header is its row #0. The report counts from 1 and also gives the line in the file.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .content {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .content li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  /* Data Loader's messages are long and must not widen the page on a phone. */
  .errors code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
